<TopicPage slug="subjects/neuroscience/advanced/memory-circuits">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The psychology ladder described memory functionally — encoding, consolidation, retrieval, the
    multi-store model. This chapter supplies the hardware: which circuits perform those operations,
    what molecular event physically embodies a memory, and how the trace moves from its birthplace to
    long-term storage. The organising discovery is that "memory" is not one faculty but a family of
    systems — declarative episodes, skills, priming, emotional conditioning — each with its own
    anatomy, dissectible by lesion, imaging and optogenetics.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the memory systems and their molecular machinery" widget="equationStep" config={memCfg} />

  <h2>H.M. and the multiple-systems doctrine</h2>
  <p>
    In 1953, surgeon William Scoville removed the medial temporal lobes (both hippocampi plus
    surrounding cortex) from Henry Molaison to cure intractable epilepsy. The epilepsy improved; the
    patient lost, permanently, the ability to form new declarative memories — severe anterograde
    amnesia — and lost most memories from the preceding decade, while childhood memories remained
    accessible. Brenda Milner's decade of study with H.M. is the most productive single-case study in
    neuroscience, and its findings structure the field to this day. Three dissociations: (1)
    <strong>short-term versus long-term</strong> — H.M. could hold a digit span conversation normally;
    working memory is prefrontal-parietal, not hippocampal. (2) <strong>Declarative versus
    procedural</strong> — he improved at mirror-drawing and rotary-pursuit tasks across days while
    each session felt brand-new to him; skills run on basal ganglia and cerebellum. (3)
    <strong>Temporal gradient</strong> — older memories survived better than recent ones; the
    hippocampus is a writer whose entries are eventually filed elsewhere. Together these established
    that the medial temporal lobe (hippocampus proper, dentate gyrus, entorhinal and perirhinal
    cortex) is necessary for <em>forming</em> episodic and semantic memories but not for <em>storing
    all of them</em> forever — the seed of systems-consolidation theory.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Kandel's reduction</strong> supplied the molecular half of the story: the sea slug
    <em>Aplysia</em> has giant, identifiable neurons and one tractable reflex. Sensitisation of the
    gill-withdrawal reflex — an example of non-associative learning — traced to specific synaptic
    changes: serotonin from a modulatory neuron increases transmitter release at a particular
    sensory-motor synapse, via cAMP and protein kinases. Kandel's gamble was that molecular logic
    would generalise up the evolutionary tree (Nobel 2000). It did — the same CREB-dependent
    transcription machinery converts short-lasting plasticity into protein-synthesis-dependent long-
    term memory in flies, mice and humans.
  </p>

  <h2>Long-term potentiation: the physical memory trace</h2>
  <p>
    At hippocampal CA3-to-CA1 synapses, a high-frequency tetanus leaves transmission potentiated for
    hours; with proper conditions, days to months. The induction mechanism is the NMDA receptor's
    coincidence-detection: the channel is blocked by magnesium at rest, so it passes calcium only
    when <em>glutamate is bound and the postsynaptic membrane is depolarised</em> — that is, when
    pre- and postsynaptic activity coincide. The calcium signal then activates kinases (CaMKII) that
    insert additional AMPA receptors into the postsynaptic density: the synapse becomes physically
    bigger and conductively stronger. This is Hebb's postulate ("cells that fire together, wire
    together") implemented in a channel, and its associative character is the neurobiological answer
    to why co-occurring features bind into a memory. <strong>Early versus late LTP</strong> matters
    for the consolidation story: early potentiation is receptor trafficking and lasts hours; late LTP
    requires gene expression — CREB-driven protein synthesis, the same molecular switch Kandel found
    — and blocking protein synthesis during the window leaves early plasticity intact while erasing
    the long-term component. Complementarily, <strong>LTD</strong> (low-frequency stimulation)
    weakens synapses through parallel mechanisms; memory storage plausibly lives in the pattern of
    strengthened and weakened weights, and spike-timing-dependent variants make the rule bidirectional
    and more realistic than pure rate-based Hebb.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> LTP is not "the memory." Potentiation is a candidate storage
    mechanism at synapses; a memory in a behaving animal is distributed across thousands of such
    changes in a recurrent circuit. The identification of <strong>engram cells</strong> — the sparse
    neuronal populations whose reactivation drives recall — closed the gap between synapse and
    behaviour, and is the field's most significant recent advance (more below).
  </p>

  <h2>Systems consolidation: the night shift</h2>
  <p>
    How does a hippocampal memory become a cortical one? The standard model: the hippocampus binds
    cortical fragments (the "index" of an episode) and, over weeks to years, neocortex gradually
    extracts and re-represents the regularities, until recall no longer needs the hippocampus. The
    mechanism is <strong>replay</strong>: during slow-wave sleep, hippocampal neurons fire compressed
    replays of waking sequences inside sharp-wave ripples, time-locked to cortical spindles and slow
    oscillations — a dialogue that appears to teach cortex. The experimental evidence in humans is
    direct: sleep (especially slow-wave-rich early nights) after learning improves retention by
    20–40% in classic paradigms, targeted memory reactivation (cueing a learned association with its
    odour or sound during sleep) selectively boosts the cued memories, and sleep deprivation after
    learning measurably impairs consolidation. Two refinements complicate the simple story: some
    cortical memories (semantic knowledge) do seem to wean off the hippocampus, while highly detailed
    episodic memories may remain hippocampus-dependent for life (Nadel and Moscovitch's multiple-trace
    theory) — the gradient's shape is still argued.
  </p>

  <h2>Reconsolidation: recall as rewriting</h2>
  <p>
    For decades retrieval was assumed passive — reading the file. Nader, Schafe and LeDoux (2000)
    showed otherwise: reactivating a fear memory in rats made it vulnerable to amnesic agents
    (protein-synthesis inhibitors in the amygdala) precisely during a window after recall — the
    memory was being <em>restored</em>, and interruption prevented restabilisation. Reconsolidation
    reframed memory as inherently constructive: each retrieval returns the trace to a labile state
    and re-files it with current context and emotion attached. This mechanism underwrites well-
    documented psychological phenomena — the misinformation effect (Loftus: leading questions and
    post-event information systematically distort testimony; roughly a third of adults can be led to
    rich false childhood memories under suggestive interviewing) — and motivates clinical
    applications: timing extinction training or propranolol within the reconsolidation window to
    weaken trauma associations rather than only suppressing them.
  </p>

  <h2>The amygdala, emotion, and forgetting diseases</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The amygdala</strong> attaches motivational weight: conditioned fear learning runs through
    its lateral-basolateral nuclei onto central outputs, and patient S.M. (Urbach-Wiethe disease,
    bilateral amygdala calcification) could not learn fear conditioning or recognise fearful faces
    while other memory systems stayed intact — the emotional mirror of H.M. Emotional arousal
    strengthens consolidation via amygdala-norepinephrine-hippocampus interactions, which is why
    flashbulb events persist — and also why they persist <em>imperfectly</em> (9/11 studies showed
    confidence in flashbulb memories rose over years while accuracy fell).
    <strong>Alzheimer's disease</strong> is the systems' failure mode: pathology (amyloid-β plaques
    and intracellular tau tangles) typically begins in entorhinal cortex and hippocampus — which is
    why the first symptom is an inability to form new episodic memories, and why the pathology then
    spreads through association cortex in a stereotyped sequence (Braak staging) that maps onto the
    clinical progression from forgetting to language and spatial disintegration. The anti-amyloid
    immunotherapy story (aducanumab's controversial approval, lecanemab and donanemab's modest
    slowing of decline) is the current frontier: measurable disease modification, meaningful side
    effects, and a still-uncertain translation from biomarker change to quality of life.
  </p>

  <h2>The other memory systems: skills, habits and priming</h2>
  <p>
    The declarative system's independence is proven by the systems that work without it.
    <strong>Procedural memory</strong> — skills and habits — runs on the basal ganglia: the
    striatum's reinforcement-learning loops (dopamine again, now as a teaching signal for
    actions rather than a prediction-error for outcomes) support motor sequences, and their
    preservation in H.M. alongside their dissolution in Parkinson's (skill learning impaired
    with episodic memory intact) form the mirror-image dissociation.
    <strong>Cerebellar learning</strong> handles supervised adaptation — classical
    conditioning of the eyeblink reflex is its model system: the cerebellum stores the
    timing-adjusted association, and cerebellar lesions abolish it while leaving declarative
    knowledge of the experiment intact. <strong>Priming and perceptual memory</strong> survive
    in perirhinal-neocortical circuits without episodic encoding. The taxonomy is not
    academic: it explains why rehabilitation after hippocampal damage trains procedures
    (which survive) rather than facts (which do not), and why expertise research (chess,
    radiology) shows knowledge crystallising out of episodes into pattern-recognition
    machinery that no longer needs the writer.
  </p>

  <h2>Emotion's modulatory arm: why arousal strengthens memory</h2>
  <p>
    The amygdala's role goes beyond fear conditioning: it modulates <em>consolidation</em> in
    the other systems. Emotional arousal releases adrenal epinephrine and cortisol, which
    engage the basolateral amygdala, which projects to the hippocampus during the
    consolidation window — the pharmacology explains the psychology: moderate arousal enhances
    memory (the Yerkes-Dodson shape), extreme stress impairs encoding and retrieval (the
    prefrontal shut-down of trauma), and beta-blocker experiments (propranolol after trauma)
    test the mechanism clinically. The same circuitry explains the flashbulb phenomenon:
    confidence and vividness track arousal, not accuracy — the 9/11 memory studies showed both
    rising confidence and falling accuracy across the years, a dissociation any
    episodic-reconstruction account predicts and pure-storage accounts cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Engram technology.</strong> Tonegawa's laboratory made memory manipulation causal:
    label neurons active during learning with light-sensitive channels, then reactivate that exact
    population later with light. Optogenetic reactivation induced freezing (the learned behaviour)
    without the cue; a mild footshock delivered during light-reactivation of a benign context's engram
    implanted a false fear memory in a place the animal had never been shocked. The conclusions are
    strong: the physical substrate of a specific memory exists as an identifiable, addressable cell
    population, and "where" a memory is stored is answerable to single-neuron precision. Competition
    between engram populations (neurons allocated by neuronal excitability at encoding — CREB-high
    neurons win allocation) explains why engaging more neurons during encoding strengthens later
    recall. <strong>Hippocampal computation.</strong> The dentate gyrus performs pattern separation
    (making similar inputs orthogonal — why two similar mornings are remembered distinctly), CA3's
    recurrent collaterals perform pattern completion (cuing a fragment recalls the whole — why one
    smell resurrects an entire scene); the trade-off between the two is the computational problem the
    circuit's architecture evolved to manage, and its failure modes are informative: pattern-separation
    deficits may underlie age-related memory interference, over-strong completion may contribute to
    intrusive recall in PTSD. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how semantic knowledge migrates from
    episodes (is cortex a slow-learning statistical summariser?); whether human replay is confirmed
    with the same certainty as rodent replay (MEG evidence is suggestive); what sleep-spindle
    disruption does to the consolidation dialogue in aging; and whether reconsolidation-based
    therapies can reliably weaken pathological memories without weakening the autobiographical self
    that depends on them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The developmental arc completes the systems view. Infantile amnesia (adults' inability to
    recall before roughly age three) reflects a hippocampus still maturing — the dentate
    gyrus's prolonged neurogenesis and slow synaptic maturation prevent stable indexing until
    the system is ready; childhood amnesia is circuit development, not erasure. Across the
    lifespan, the systems dissociate in aging: episodic and source memory decline first
    (hippocampal-prefrontal networks), while semantic knowledge and procedural skill remain
    robust late (cortical and striatal systems) — which is why an elderly pianist may play
    fluently while forgetting where the sheet music is. And sleep architecture shifts with
    age: reduced slow-wave amplitude weakens the consolidation dialogue, one mechanism linking
    aging's sleep changes to its memory changes. The systems taxonomy, built from lesions, is
    thus also a lifespan map — which system carries which decades of the autobiographical
    record.
  </p>
  <p>
    Retrieval practice closes the loop from circuits to study habits: testing recall — rather
    than rereading — strengthens the retrieval pathways themselves (the testing effect, among
    the largest and best-replicated findings in applied memory research), and spacing exploits
    the consolidation and reconsolidation windows by forcing the trace through repeated
    labile-restable cycles. The neurobiology and the study advice are the same fact at two
    levels of description.
  </p>

